# Why did Darwin wait twenty years?
subtitle: "Darwin had natural selection by 1838 but published in 1859: what he did with those twenty years, and the letter that ended them"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Algenib | a gravelly historian telling a story; warm and gripping, natural pace","take":"lines"}

## Why did Darwin wait twenty years?
domain: {"x":[1829,1865],"y":[0,10]}
sources:
    - id: vanwyhe2007
      title: 'Mind the gap: did Darwin avoid publishing his theory for many years?'
      authors: van Wyhe J
      year: 2007
      finding: 'Argues that the twenty-year delay out of fear is a myth: Darwin worked openly on species and evidence and meant to publish after finishing his barnacle work.'

    arrow tl #8f887c from.x 1829.5 from.y 4 to.x 1864 to.y 4 hidden true
    curve beagle #2f6b8f expr "4" x_from 1831.9 x_to 1836.8 style.stroke_width 8 hidden true
    text l_beagle "Beagle, 1831–36" above beagle gap 12 #2f6b8f font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_idea at.x 1838 at.y 4 hidden true
    text l_idea "1838: the idea" below p_idea gap 12 font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_origin #b5482e at.x 1859.9 at.y 4 hidden true
    text l_origin "1859: the Origin" above p_origin gap 12 #b5482e font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow span dashed #b5482e from.x 1838 from.y 6.5 to.x 1859.9 to.y 6.5 hidden true
    text l_span "21 years" above span gap 12 #b5482e cites ["vanwyhe2007"] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot p_essay #8a5fa8 at.x 1844.5 at.y 4 hidden true
    text l_essay "1844: the essay" above p_essay gap 12 #8a5fa8 font_size 30 hidden true
    curve barn #8a5fa8 expr "4" x_from 1846 x_to 1854 style.stroke_width 8 hidden true
    text l_barn "1846–54: barnacles" below barn gap 12 #8a5fa8 font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_wallace at.x 1858.5 at.y 4 hidden true
    text l_wallace "1858: Wallace" below p_wallace gap 62 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon i_ship #2f6b8f size 72 x 239 y 558 of ship hidden true
    icon i_essay #8a5fa8 size 72 x 469 y 558 of scroll hidden true
    icon i_shell #8a5fa8 size 72 x 593 y 558 of shell hidden true
    icon i_mail #8f887c size 64 x 905 y 235 of envelope hidden true
    icon i_book #b5482e size 72 x 831 y 558 of book-open hidden true
    path tr0 thick #3b3a36 points [[1837.6,7.8],[1837.6,8.3]] data true hidden true
    path tr1 thick #3b3a36 points [[1837.6,8.3],[1836.7,8.9]] data true hidden true
    path tr2 thick #3b3a36 points [[1837.6,8.3],[1838.5,8.9]] data true hidden true
    path tr3 thick #3b3a36 points [[1836.7,8.9],[1836.3,9.25]] data true hidden true
    path tr4 thick #3b3a36 points [[1836.7,8.9],[1837.1,9.25]] data true hidden true
    path tr5 thick #3b3a36 points [[1838.5,8.9],[1838.9,9.25]] data true hidden true
    group tree_sk members ["tr0","tr1","tr2","tr3","tr4","tr5"] hidden true

    card title "Why did Darwin wait twenty years?"

Why did Charles Darwin wait twenty years to publish natural selection? He had it worked out by 1838, and the book came out in 1859.
    draw tl

Here are those years to scale. First the Beagle's voyage round the world, 1831 to 1836: finches, fossils, and growing doubts that species are fixed.
    draw beagle l_beagle i_ship

Home in 1837, he sketched a branching tree in a notebook under the words 'I think'. A year later, reading Malthus, he had the mechanism.
    draw tree_sk p_idea l_idea

The book, On the Origin of Species, came out in November 1859.
    draw p_origin l_origin i_book

Twenty-one years apart: most of the line. The usual story is that he feared the scandal, and hid the idea in a drawer.
    draw span l_span

    pause 0.4

But the drawer was full. By 1844 he had written the theory out as a long essay, with a letter asking his wife Emma to publish it if he died.
    draw p_essay l_essay i_essay

Then eight years on barnacles, 1846 to 1854: the expert standing, and the habit of patient evidence, that a heretical claim would need.
    draw barn l_barn i_shell

In June 1858, deep in a huge book on species, he opened a letter from Alfred Russel Wallace in the Malay Archipelago. It described the same idea.
    draw p_wallace l_wallace i_mail

Within two weeks both men's papers were read together in London, and the Origin, which Darwin called a mere abstract, followed the next year.
    highlight p_wallace p_origin

So the idea was finished early. What filled the gap was evidence, and what ended it was a rival.
    highlight span

Historians still argue over his motives, and some doubt it was a delay at all: for a naturalist then, twenty years of evidence was simply thoroughness.
    point at.ref l_span gesture circle

    quiz "How much of his theory had Darwin written down by 1844?"
        right: A long essay, with instructions to publish it if he died: the gap was not an empty drawer but twenty years of gathering evidence.
        * Nothing: it was all in his head
        + A long essay setting it out
        * The finished Origin
---
